**Reasoning for Scores:**

*   **price\_usd**: Rated 0.00. The price for a mid-range TDI Transporter T6.1 (like a Trendline or Commerce Plus) is typically around the $35,000 market average benchmark when considering VAT and potential options (Source 3, 4, 12, 28).
*   **total\_cost\_estimate\_5yr\_usd**: Rated 0.00. As a popular and relatively reliable commercial vehicle with decent fuel economy, the 5-year ownership cost is expected to be around the market average, balancing depreciation and running costs (Source 3, 20).
*   **insurance\_usd\_yr**: Rated 0.00. Insurance groups for the Transporter T6.1 vary widely (5-47 or 20-47 depending on the scale used) depending on specification and use (Source 26, 29, 30, 31, 32). Mid-range TDI models are typically in average groups, averaging out to the national benchmark compared to the full range of vehicles.
*   **maint\_usd\_yr**: Rated 0.00. Volkswagen maintenance costs are generally considered average compared to the overall market (Source 3, 11, 15, 28).
*   **repair\_risk\_score**: Rated 0.50. The Transporter T6.1 has a strong reputation for reliability, particularly in commercial use, placing it above average compared to some less reliable brands (Source 13, 15, 20, 24, 28, 29, 35, 36). While some owner reports mention specific issues (AdBlue, EGR, oil consumption on certain engines), its overall standing is well above average reliability (Source 12, 13, 15, 24, 29, 35, 36, 40).
*   **hp**: Rated -0.30. A common TDI engine option is the 150 PS (148 hp) 2.0 TDI (Source 2, 5, 7, 9, 11, 14, 15, 16, 17, 21, 23, 33, 37, 38). This is below the 200 hp average car benchmark.
*   **torque**: Rated 0.30. The 150 PS engine produces 340 Nm (approx 250 lb-ft) (Source 2, 5, 7, 9, 14, 15, 17, 22, 23, 37). This is above the 190 lb-ft average car benchmark.
*   **cylinder\_count**: Rated 0.00. The 2.0L engine is a 4-cylinder, which is average for most vehicles today (Source 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14, 15, 17, 21, 22, 23, 32, 33).
*   **engine\_displacement**: Rated 0.00. The 2.0L engine displacement is average for many vehicles (Source 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 14, 15, 17, 21, 22, 23, 32, 33).
*   **accel\_0\_60\_sec**: Rated -0.60. 0-60 mph times for the 150 PS engine are around 11-12 seconds (Source 7, 11, 17), significantly slower than the 7.5-second average car benchmark.
*   **top\_speed**: Rated -0.70. Top speeds for the 150 PS engine are around 111-113 mph (Source 5, 7, 9, 11, 17, 21), well below the 150 mph average car benchmark.
*   **drive\_type**: Rated 0.00. FWD is standard (Source 5, 7, 9, 11, 14, 15, 17, 23, 25, 27, 28), but 4MOTION AWD is available as an option on the 150PS engine (Source 11, 14, 15, 16, 17, 22, 25, 36), placing the model range in the average category.
*   **transmission\_type**: Rated 0.00. Both 6-speed manual and 7-speed DSG automatic transmissions are available with the 150PS engine (Source 2, 3, 7, 8, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17, 21, 22, 23, 29, 33, 36). DSG is a Dual-Clutch Transmission (0.00).
*   **offroad\_ability**: Rated 0.80. The Transporter has limited offroad capability compared to dedicated vehicles, placing it towards the higher end of this scale as per the guide's examples (Source 4, 5, 8).
*   **braking\_dist\_ft**: Rated 0.00, assuming braking performance is around the average for a vehicle of its size and type.
*   **size**: Rated 0.30. It is a large van, available in Short Wheelbase (SWB) (4904mm) and Long Wheelbase (LWB) (5304mm) (Source 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 34, 35, 36, 37, 39). This is significantly longer than average cars but not as massive as the largest SUVs/trucks.
*   **wheelbase**: Rated 0.40. Available in SWB (3000mm) and LWB (3400mm) wheelbases (Source 3, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 21, 23, 24, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 34, 35, 36, 37, 39), exceeding the average car wheelbase.
*   **body\_style**: Rated 1.00. It is available as a panel van, Kombi, Shuttle, etc. (Source 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20, 25, 29, 32, 34, 35). 5 doors is a common configuration for passenger/crew versions. The guide scores 5-door vans at +1.00.
*   **seats**: Rated 0.00. While panel vans have 2-3 seats (Source 2, 8, 9, 10, 14, 15, 16, 20, 25, 32, 33), Kombi/Shuttle versions commonly offer 5 or more seats (Source 10, 14, 16, 20, 25, 32, 33), making 5 seats (the average benchmark) a common configuration across the model.
*   **cargo\_space**: Rated 1.00. Offers substantial cargo volume (5.8 m³ for SWB, up to 9.3 m³ for LWB high roof) (Source 7, 14, 16, 17, 23, 25, 27, 28, 30, 34, 37, 39), far exceeding typical cars and SUVs.
*   **rear\_space**: Rated 0.00. Rear legroom varies significantly by configuration (panel van has none, Kombi/Shuttle versions have decent space), averaging out to the benchmark.
*   **ground\_clearance\_in**: Rated 0.20. Around 7.9 inches (201mm) (Source 7, 8, 11, 21, 26, 27, 37), higher than most cars but lower than dedicated SUVs or off-roaders.
*   **turn\_radius\_ft**: Rated -0.30. Turning circle is 11.9m (approx 39 ft) for SWB and 13.2m (approx 43.3 ft) for LWB (Source 7, 11, 17, 21, 27, 31, 34, 39). This is wider than the 36 ft average car benchmark.
*   **mpg**: Rated 0.20. Fuel economy for the 150 PS engine is around 35-40 mpg (Source 7, 11, 15, 19, 21, 23, 29, 35, 39). This is better than the 30 mpg average car benchmark, especially considering the vehicle's size.
*   **co2\_g\_km**: Rated 0.00. CO2 emissions are around 161-206 g/km depending on engine/config (Source 2, 6, 7, 15, 16, 18, 21, 25, 28, 38, 39), which is around the average for non-electric vehicles.
